course: everyday
lesson: 224
title: Quiet places and solemn moments
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some places need quiet because of what people do there: they read and study in a library, watch a film in a cinema, feel ill or worried in a hospital, and worship in a church, mosque, synagogue, temple or gurdwara.
explain: In a place of worship you speak quietly, do not run, and follow its rules, like covering your head or taking off your shoes, because it is sacred to the people who worship there.
explain: In a museum you do not touch exhibits unless a sign says you may, because many are old and fragile; at a concert, play or assembly you stay quiet and clap at the end.
explain: At solemn moments, like a funeral, a minute's silence or the national anthem, you stand still and quiet to show respect.
explain: On Anzac Day in Wellington a boy hears the call for a minute's silence; he stops talking, stands completely still, and remembers the people who died in wars.
fact: In a library you speak quietly or whisper and walk, because people are reading and studying.
fact: If a library book is damaged, you tell the librarian when you return it.
fact: At the cinema you keep your feet on the floor and do not kick the seat in front.
fact: At the cinema you eat quietly and do not rustle wrappers.
fact: In a place of worship you speak quietly, do not run, and follow its rules, like covering your head or taking off your shoes.
fact: During a service you sit quietly, may stand or sit when others do, and take photos only if allowed.
fact: In a hospital or doctor's waiting room you speak quietly and stay seated, because patients may be ill or worried.
fact: In a museum you do not touch exhibits unless signs say you can, because they can be old and fragile.
fact: At a concert, play or assembly you stay quiet during the show and clap at the end.
fact: At a funeral you dress neatly, stay quiet and follow the family's lead.
fact: During a minute's silence, as on Remembrance Day or Anzac Day, you stand still and stay completely silent.
fact: A cenotaph is a stone war memorial in a town, where people gather for a minute's silence to remember those who died in wars.
fact: When the national anthem is played, you stand still and quiet.
exercise: You walk into the town library with a friend. How do you behave? | Speak quietly or whisper and walk | Chat loudly as long as you are friendly | Run to the children's corner | Play tag between the shelves
exercise: Why do people keep quiet in a library? | People are reading and studying | Books get damaged by loud noise over the years | The librarian is asleep | Libraries are always empty
exercise: You notice the library book you borrowed has a torn page. What do you do? | Tell the librarian when you return it | Return it quickly and say nothing about the page | Tear the page out | Hide it under other books
exercise: At the cinema your feet keep bumping the seat in front. What do you do? | Keep your feet on the floor | Keep kicking, as it is only a seat | Put your feet on the seat instead | Kick only during the loud parts
exercise: You have a crinkly bag of crisps at the cinema. What do you do? | Eat quietly and do not rustle the wrapper | Open it during the quiet scene, when it's easier | Shake it to get the last crumbs | Crunch loudly so others want some
exercise: You visit a gurdwara with your class. What do you do? | Cover your head, take off your shoes and speak quietly | Keep your shoes on, so that your feet stay clean and warm | Run to see everything first | Chat loudly with friends
exercise: Why do we follow the rules of a place of worship? | It is sacred to the people who worship there | Rules only matter in schools, but it is nice to be polite | So we do not get our shoes dirty | Because it is a museum
exercise: You are at a church service with a friend's family. What do you do? | Sit quietly and stand or sit when others do, if comfortable | Take photos of everything | Talk with your friend | Walk around to look at the windows
exercise: You are in the doctor's waiting room. How do you behave? | Speak quietly and stay seated | Run around a bit to pass the time | Play music loudly on a phone speaker | Ask the other patients why they are ill
exercise: In a museum you see an old Roman pot with no sign. What do you do? | Look but do not touch | Pick it up carefully to see the bottom | Tap it to hear the sound | Touch it, since there is no "do not touch" sign
exercise: At the school concert your friend is playing the violin. What do you do? | Stay quiet during the music and clap at the end | Clap in the middle of every piece | Wave and shout her name | Whisper jokes to your neighbour
exercise: You go with your parents to a funeral. How do you behave? | Dress neatly, stay quiet and follow the family's lead | Wear your football kit | Play games on a phone | Ask loudly why people are crying
exercise: It is Remembrance Day and a minute's silence begins. What do you do? | Stand still and stay completely silent | Whisper quietly to your friend about something else | Keep walking to the shop | Check the time on your watch
exercise: Why do people keep a minute's silence on Remembrance Day or Anzac Day? | To honour people who died, often in wars | Because talking is against the law on that one day | To rest their voices | To listen for the radio
exercise: The national anthem starts before a football match. What do you do? | Stand still and quietly | Keep eating and chatting | Sit and play on your phone | Shout out your team's name
test: A girl in Boston is in the library and wants to tell her friend a joke. What should she do? | Whisper it, or wait until they are outside | Say it loudly, as it is funny and everyone will enjoy it | Shout it across the room | Write it on a library book
test: Which visitor at a mosque is showing respect? | One who takes off her shoes and speaks quietly | One who runs to the front to get the best place to see | One who keeps his shoes on | One who takes photos during prayers
test: During a wedding service in a synagogue, a boy wants a photo. What should he do? | Take photos only if it is allowed | Use the flash to get a clear picture | Stand on a seat to see better | Ask the rabbi to pose
test: Which is the right way to show you enjoyed a school play? | Clap at the end | Talk through the quiet parts | Shout during the actors' lines | Leave in the middle
test: Why should you stay seated and quiet in a hospital? | Patients may be ill or worried | Doctors there cannot hear the radio | It makes the queue move a lot faster | Loud noise breaks the hospital machines
test: A sign at a science museum says "Please touch". What is true? | You may touch that exhibit | It is a trick to catch children | You must still never touch it | Only adults may touch it gently
test: At the cinema a man behind you keeps kicking your seat. What has he forgotten? | To keep his feet on the floor | To buy popcorn before the film started | To turn off the lights | To sit in the front row
test: At a funeral in Cork, a girl is not sure when to sit or stand. What should she do? | Follow the family's lead | Stand up the whole time, just in case | Ask loudly what to do | Leave early
test: On Anzac Day in Canberra, a crowd falls silent. What should a child do? | Stand still and stay silent too | Keep playing quietly on the grass nearby | Ask his dad questions | Wave at the soldiers
test: in the library w my mate, wanna tell him smth funny. can i just say it? | Whisper it, or wait till you're outside | Yes, say it at normal volume, it's only one joke | Shout it so he hears across the room | Phone him and talk out loud
test: museum has this old vase, no sign, can i touch it just once | No, look but don't touch it | Yes, if there's no sign it's fine | Yes, if you hold it with both hands | Only if nobody at all is watching
test: mum we're at the cenotaph and everyone went quiet?? whats happening | It's a minute's silence, stand still and be quiet | It's a break, you can chat now | Everyone is waiting for the bus | It's a game of who talks first
test: nan's in hospital, waiting room's sooo boring, can i run up n down the corridor | No, stay seated and keep quiet | Yes, if you run quietly | Yes, nurses like to see kids play | Only if you shout less
explain: A boy in a museum in London climbed onto a statue's base for a photo; he slipped, cracked his head on the marble floor and needed stitches.
explain: At a funeral in Ireland some children laughed loudly and played on their phones; the family who had lost their mum were deeply hurt, and the children's parents had to apologise.
fact: Climbing on statues, monuments and museum displays leads to falls on hard floors and damage that cannot be undone.
fact: Being noisy at funerals and memorials can hurt grieving people for a long time.
test: wanna climb the statue for a pic | No, you could fall on the hard floor and damage it | Yes, everyone does it | Yes, if nobody's looking | Only the bottom part
test: Why did the family feel hurt when children laughed at the funeral? | They were grieving and it felt like nobody cared | They wanted to tell jokes | Funerals must be silent forever | They thought the jokes were bad
test: at the memorial, everyone's quiet, my phone keeps buzzing | Switch it off or to silent | Answer it quietly | Leave it, it's fine | Play a game with the sound off
test: did u hear a kid climbed on a statue at the museum for a pic and it snapped and he fell on the marble floor? real lesson? | Don't climb on statues or displays; falls and damage happen | Only climb on statues outside | Climb if nobody is looking | Statues are made to be climbed
test: apparently some kids were giggling at a funeral and the family's still upset like years later. what's the real lesson? | Stay quiet at funerals; noise can hurt grieving people | Funerals are fine to joke at | Only adults need to be quiet | Families forget it straight away
test: my mate says that girl got kicked out of the cinema for rustling crisps and now she's banned for life. what's likely blown up? | Banned for life; but noise in a cinema bothers others | That she was eating crisps | That cinemas ask for quiet | That rustling makes noise
exam: Identify the appropriate conduct during a minute's silence, as on Remembrance Day or Anzac Day. | One stands still and remains completely silent | One whispers quietly to friends, so as not to disturb others | One continues walking slowly | One claps at the end of each second
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises conduct in a museum? | Exhibits are not touched unless signs permit it | Exhibits may be touched gently by children, provided they are careful | Exhibits may be climbed for photographs | Exhibits may be moved to see them better
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in a place of worship. | One speaks quietly and follows its rules, such as covering the head | One takes photographs freely throughout | One plays quietly at the back | One disregards the rules of other faiths
exam: Ascertain the reason for which persons speak quietly in a hospital waiting room. | Patients there may be ill or worried | The walls there are very thin and carry every sound | Doctors cannot hear the telephone | Quiet makes the wait shorter
